Ovarian Hyperstimulation Syndrome (OHSS)
Hydration, Fluid and Volume abnormalities Third-Spacing
Massive ascites/rapid weight gain, Pronounced oliguria, Hemoconcentration (hematocrit >45%)

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: HFV-031
Reason for Visit: Acute onset of severe abdominal swelling, shortness of breath, and markedly decreased urine output following fertility treatments, requiring immediate after-hours assessment for third-spacing and impending shock.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage documented a patient undergoing in vitro fertilization presenting with sudden, massive abdominal distension, severe pelvic pain, and an inability to lie flat due to breathlessness. This presentation indicated critical fluid shifting and intravascular volume depletion that demanded an urgent medical assessment to evaluate hemodynamic stability and prevent deep vein thrombosis or renal failure prior to standard morning hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: Massive stomach swelling, severe pelvic pain, and shortness of breath. Patient states, My stomach has ballooned out over the last two days, I am constantly nauseous, and I have barely passed any urine today despite drinking liters of water.
History of Present Illness: Symptoms commenced progressively 72 hours following ovulation induction therapy. The patient experienced a rapid onset of massive ascites and progressive abdominal distension, accompanied by a sudden and significant weight gain of 5 kilograms over three days. This was sequentially followed by severe lower abdominal and pelvic pain due to markedly enlarged ovaries. Today, the patient developed pronounced oliguria despite adequate oral fluid intake, and nausea with recurrent vomiting preventing further oral hydration. Over the last four hours, bilateral pleural effusions have clinically evolved, leading to dyspnea and orthopnea. The patient feels highly tachycardic and dizzy upon standing, reflecting severe intravascular volume depletion. Generalized peripheral edema is now prominent.
Functional Status: Functionally limited, resting in a seated position to breathe comfortably.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of venous thromboembolism and renal compromise; spouse is available for support and monitoring.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 105/65 mmHg | HR: 115 bpm | Temp: 36.9 C | RR: 22 bpm | SpO2: 95% | Wt: 70.0 Kg (Baseline 65.0 Kg)
General: Patient appears uncomfortable, pale, visibly distended, and tachypneic when supine.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular: Tachycardia is marked. A postural drop in blood pressure is clinically evident. Capillary refill is delayed (3 seconds). Respiratory: Decreased breath sounds and dullness to percussion at the lung bases bilaterally, consistent with early pleural effusions. Gastrointestinal: The diagnostic triad is clinically profound. There is massive ascites confirmed by shifting dullness and rapid weight gain. Pronounced oliguria is confirmed historically. High clinical suspicion exists for hemoconcentration (hematocrit >45%) pending urgent pathology. The abdomen is tense and tender in the lower quadrants. Genitourinary: Ovaries are presumed massively enlarged; deep pelvic palpation deferred to ultrasound to prevent ovarian torsion or rupture.
Environmental Observations: Ground floor access; comfortable recliner chair utilized for positioning.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Recent administration of human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG) trigger shot confirmed.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Ovarian Hyperstimulation Syndrome (OHSS) - Status: Severe Decompensation
Differentials:

Pelvic Inflammatory Disease - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of fever or purulent cervical discharge, alongside the clear temporal relationship to fertility medications and massive third-spacing.

Ovarian Torsion - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Bilateral, diffuse, progressively worsening pelvic pain and systemic fluid overload, rather than sudden, unilateral, sharp, intermittent ischemic pain (though torsion remains a complication risk).

Ectopic Pregnancy - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of massive ascites, pleural effusions, and profound hemoconcentration, which define OHSS rather than an isolated tubal rupture.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with severe iatrogenic third-spacing of fluid from the intravascular space into the peritoneal and pleural cavities, directly triggered by ovulation induction. The profound oliguria, tachycardia, and massive sudden weight gain confirm critical intravascular volume depletion and hemoconcentration, placing the patient at immediate risk for acute kidney injury and deep vein thrombosis. Delaying this assessment until the next in-hours period carried a significant risk of irreversible end-organ damage or pulmonary compromise. Urgent after-hours physical assessment was essential to diagnose the severity of the fluid shift, rule out acute ovarian rupture, and immediately coordinate urgent admission for intravenous albumin, strict fluid balance monitoring, and thromboprophylaxis under the care of a specialist gynecologist. The expected investigation results include significant hemoconcentration with a hematocrit >45% and a transvaginal ultrasound demonstrating massive bilaterally enlarged ovaries with multiple cysts that would assist in confirming the diagnosis upon hospital admission.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed Ondansetron 4mg sublingually for nausea control. Directed patient to remain semi-recumbent. Coordinated immediate referral and transfer to the local tertiary women's hospital emergency department for specialist admission.
Positive Behavioral Change: To maintain adequate blood flow to your vital organs while awaiting transport, you must commit to drinking small, 50ml sips of an electrolyte solution every 15 minutes. Use a specific small cup. When you successfully finish the cup without vomiting, explicitly recognize that you are actively keeping your blood vessels open and protecting your kidneys. This direct physical preservation will reward your strict adherence to the sipping schedule.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Immediate handover to Gynecology and Emergency Medicine teams.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Transport to the hospital is currently being arranged. If you experience sudden, sharp, unilateral pelvic pain, begin coughing up blood, or develop unilateral leg swelling, call 000 immediately as these indicate severe complications.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: This is a severe complication of fertility treatment requiring inpatient monitoring, fluid management, and potential drainage of the abdominal fluid.
Clinical Course: The fertility medications have caused your blood vessels to become leaky. Fluid is pouring out of your bloodstream into your abdomen and lungs, leaving your actual blood volume dangerously low and thick. The hospital will use specialized intravenous fluids to pull that water back into your veins.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not perform any sudden movements, twisting, or bending. Your ovaries are massively enlarged and heavy, making them highly susceptible to twisting and cutting off their own blood supply.

PATIENT EDUCATION

